ready: 1
kind: rule
id: trust.rule.entity-trust
display_id: RULE-TRUST-ENTITY
module: trust
title: How trust in an entity is computed
summary: >-
  Trust in an entity combines direct evidence, decays toward neutral without new evidence, and passes
  through other entities only a few steps deep and never above the weakest link.
owner: crates/kernle-core/src/trust/assess.rs::compute
inputs:
- name: assessment
  source: The entity's domain scores and the episodes cited as evidence
  source_kind: stored
- name: age
  source: Days since the assessment last changed
  source_kind: derived
- name: chain
  source: Assessments of the entities along a trust chain
  source_kind: stored
constants:
- trust.const.decay-half-life
- trust.const.chain-depth
- trust.const.self-floor
calculations:
- name: direct trust
  expr: >-
    for the asked domain (general when the domain has no score), the evidence-weighted mean of set and
    observed scores, each episode weighted by its salience
- name: decayed trust
  expr: 0.5 + (direct trust - 0.5) × 0.5 ^ (days since last change ÷ decay half-life)
- name: transitive trust
  expr: >-
    product of decayed trust along the chain, capped at the lowest link, for chains of at most the chain
    depth
- name: self trust
  expr: the agent's trust in its own observations never falls below the self-trust floor
then: The computed trust is returned with the evidence it rests on and the chain used
default: 0.5 (neutral) for an entity with no evidence
rationale: >-
  Decay toward neutral keeps one bad interaction from defining an entity forever, and keeps old praise
  from vouching for an entity that has changed. Bounding chain depth keeps "a friend of a friend of a
  friend" from carrying meaningful trust.
